WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  have you ever thought about  how each of us got to where we are today  i've often asked myself what are all the  things that i did right to get into an  ivy league college  and then earn a six figure salary as a  21 year old  well turns out all i really had to do  right  was to be born into the right household  and right neighborhood  from then on the odds were in my favor  unlike many of us here today  tashana murphy was not so lucky  taishana grew up in new york city public  housing until the age of 18  when she was gunned down in the hallway  outside her home  her friends were wrapped up in a gang  rivalry  she had suffered an asthma attack the  day before  something that disproportionately  affects children living in substandard  housing  and couldn't run away fast enough  she was a star basketball player and  would have been the first  person in her family to go to college  through a community development  corporation i was working with at the  time  i met tishana's dad we gathered  in a makeshift meeting space in west  harlem  a block away from where tijana was  killed  he stood in front of me  side by side with the mother of  t'shauna's killer  an unfathomable alliance their pain  suppressed by their resolve to end the  vicious cycle  of losing their sons and daughters to  early graves  literally or figuratively at the hands  of the nypd together  they pleaded for after-school programs  a basketball court a new community  center  all of which i took for granted growing  up  unfortunately for children living in  poverty  tashana's story is the rule  not the exception because where you grow  up  makes you who you are  this is not a talk about the failures of  the criminal justice system though  and that certainly plays a role  this is about a feature with better  neighborhoods  as the pathway to change let's start  with evidence  harvard economist raj chetty used tax  records  to track virtually every american born  in the late 70s  to early 80s there are three findings i  want to share with you  first poverty is intergenerational  a child born poor will only have a 7.5  percent chance  of becoming rich  second children born into poverty  are more affected by where they grow up  some places lifted them out of poverty  but most trapped them but those rare  places  that fostered upward mobility have four  characteristics in common  they were integrated had a mix of  incomes and races  have better schools greater social  capital  and more stable family structures  and finally access to opportunity  is unequal fewer than five percent  of black children grew up in  environments that better their long-term  prospects  and it's not just about economic  outcomes  where you grow up also affects the most  fundamental  of measures life expectancy  today in chicago just a few subway stops  can result in a 16-year difference in  life expectancy at birth  in new orleans a baby born in the  poorest neighborhood will live  25 years less than a baby born just  four miles away in the most affluent  neighborhood  i have to admit it wasn't until i was a  freshman at brown  after i had spent time tutoring students  in olneyville in providence  that began to scratch the surface of  understanding  how environment can undermine a child's  long-term prospects  the tale of two cities plays out in  providence  two up on college hill  lies my alma mater nestled amongst  quintessential new england homes frozen  in time  under the protective blanket of historic  preservation laws  down the hill lies olneyville  a largely hispanic neighborhood where  more than 40 percent of families live  below the poverty line  olneyville was hit hard by the subprime  mortgage crisis  block after block of foreclosures houses  boarded up  yards overgrown with weeds  shattered glass and broken siding strung  all over  from vandalism and squatting by drug  users  the despondent air outside was stifling  if this was the reality that i woke up  to  and saw outside the classroom window  every single day  the concentration of neglect  what kind of future would i dare to  imagine for myself  would i still have been able to beat the  odds if the odds  say that there's a greater chance for  incarceration than graduating high  school  these questions have stayed with me for  the past 12 years  driving my career decisions from  studying urban planning to leaving my  consulting job  to work on bringing more affordable  housing schools and child care centers  to underserved areas of new york city  looking ahead i want to offer a new  paradigm  from my own childhood from the age  of 9 to 19 i lived through what it meant  to be poor after my family immigrated to  the u.s  my dad was a phd student supporting us  on ten thousand dollars a year  i received free and reduced lunch at  school  and my mom's weekly ritual was counting  tips  she received from waiting tables at the  local chinese restaurant  one of the few places that would take  informal workers like her  yet the affordable housing community  that i lived in  bred connection and promise  outside of every building there were  playgrounds with swing sets  that became the go-to place for heart to  hearts with my best friend  long into the night there was a  community center where my friends and i  would gather for movies  crafts and english practice with  volunteer tutors  families moved a little bit further  after saving enough to afford their own  town homes or houses  but that meant my parents could always  count on having someone to help out with  the bills on a rainy day  and i could count on continuing to see  my friends at school  i recognized that my option of living in  affordable housing affiliated with  university where my dad was getting his  phd  is in itself a privilege  that's exactly the problem  the ability to live in environments that  better our long-term prospects  shouldn't just be for the few with the  right family background or skin color  it should be for everyone  join me in realizing this vision  of course i don't expect you to pursue  careers in community development or  urban policy as i have though i'd be  thrilled  and if you haven't already done so i'd  encourage you  to give back to communities that you  have connections to  instead i want all of us to re-examine  whether some of the things that we hold  dear are actually perpetuating the  status quo  our desire to preserve peace and  character in our own neck of the woods  could that be shutting out entire  classes of people and skin colors  our drive to give our children the best  education possible  could that be depriving local public  schools of much needed resources  and political capital and finally  our affinity for like-minded individuals  could that be numbing our compassion  towards the plight  of others are we  all complicit can we each  contribute more  our prospects in life shouldn't be left  to the sheer  chance of the neighborhood or household  that we're born into because where you  grow up  makes you who you are we need to work  together to  end the tide to turn the tide of the  punishing effects  of intergenerational poverty we need to  end the tale of two cities join me  in changing destiny one neighborhood  at a time

